  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The nor'easter is sitting well offshore, with \nthe RAP MSLP at a minimum of 1009mb. As high builds in the wake \nof the cold front, strong CAA and gusty nrly winds continue, \naided by the deepening low offshore. There continues to be high\nconfidence that a strong Nor'easter will strengthen and linger \nwell off the Carolina coast through early/mid this work week. \nStrong winds, high waves, and coastal flooding as well as \nextremely dangerous marine conditions will continue. \n\nThere is still model uncertainty beyond early week on where the\nlow pressure area will be, but the trend in 18/00Z global \nguidance including ensemble packet has continued to show a shift\na bit further offshore. Of note, quick strengthening of the low\nas it meanders offshore in addition to a slight westward shift \nis being indicated by CAMs and some synoptic guidance Monday. To\naccount for this, wind gusts have nudged up a bit Monday. \nFurther increases are possible if future guidance locks in on \nthis trend. Confidence in coastal impacts is high, as long \nduration and long fetch nerly winds will bring large waves and \npile up water along the coast, leading to coastal flooding well \ninto the week.\n\nHigh winds on the coast still expected. Have dropped the Wind\nAdvisory for Mainland Hyde with the most recent forecast keeping\nthe strongest winds along OBX. We will have to watch model\ntrends in case the low deepens substantially and shifts a bit\nwest Monday into Tuesday, in which case chances of stronger \nwind gusts near 50mph would increase.\n\n2-4 foot above ground level coastal flooding will be possible\nwith minor to moderate impacts throughout the Outer Banks, Core\nBanks and Downeast Carteret County. Impacts are expected to \nboth sound and oceanside locales with potentially significant \nimpacts to vulnerable areas on the ocean side. A mix of coastal \nflood headlines is in effect to cover these hazards. One recent\naddition to the suite of coastal headlines was to issue a\nCoastal Flood Advisory for Tyrrell County. The Sheriff's office\nreported HWY 94 is flooded, with additional public reports of\ndeep water along that stretch of highway. This is likely due to\nwater piling up on the southern end of the Alligator River with\nstrong, gale-force winds blowing straight south down the length\nof the river.\n\nAdditionally, a high surf advisory is in effect for the Outer \nBanks with breaking waves of generally 8-14 feet expected in the\nsurf zone. A moderate to high risk of rip currents is also \nexpected through much of the week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Temperatures have cooled substantially with the\nnortherly surge. Maximum temperatures through the period are \nexpected to remain in the 60-70 degree range with cooler values \ninland and warmer temperatures near the coast. Overnight lows \nwill range from the 40s inland just after the frontal passage to\nthe 50s by the end of the week with coastal locations in the \n50s early in the forecast period to near 60 by the end of next \nweek. Winds will remain breezy, particularly in the afternoons \ndepending upon the eventual track of the offshore nor'easter. \n\n\nAs the low meanders offshore, some wrap around rain may\noccasionally sneak back onshore, with best chances on the OBX \nover the next several days.",
